  "query_preview": "..(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 429 AM EST FRI NOV 20 2026\n\nAt the start of the long term period, midlevel troughing will be \ncentered over the High Plains, with southwesterly flow ahead of \nthe trough axis. To the north, a closed upper level low will be \nlocated over the Canadian Prairies; meanwhile, another shortwave \nwill be initially centered over the New Mexico/Colorado state \nline. At the surface, an area of low pressure will be located over\nOntario, with a second low near the Oklahoma and Texas \nPanhandles. Connecting these features, a cold front will be draped\nthrough the Midwest. Concurrently, an additional low will be \nsituated off the coast of the Southeast. With time, this coastal \nlow will continue its northward progression. Meanwhile, the cold \nfront extending from the Canadian cyclone will push east toward \nKentucky. \n\nOn Sunday, winds will initially be light and southeasterly. \nHowever, as the day progresses, the Canadian surface low will \ntranslate southeast somewhat into the Great Lakes, with winds \nbecoming more southwesterly around this feature. This will \ninitiate modest moisture return in the forecast area, with \ndewpoints climbing the highest in our southwestern counties. Total\ncolumn moisture will also increase throughout the day, with LREF \nmean PW values climbing into the 0.7 to 0.9 inch range ahead of \nthe front, roughly the 80th percentile compared to climatology. \nInterestingly, some deterministic guidance does depict \nprecipitation from the coastal low making its way to far eastern \nKentucky (mainly Pike County), although the NBM has kept PoPs just\nbelow mentionable at this time. There is still significant \nuncertainty over the track of this low, with questions regarding \nwhether the system can gain tropical characteristics and how it \nwill interact with a shortwave traversing the Southeast. Should a \nmore inland precipitation shield come to fruition, the main impact\nwould be a quicker increase in dewpoints for the far eastern \nportion of the forecast area, with even the most inland solutions \nfavoring very minimal, if any, local QPF with this system. High \nclouds associated with the coastal low may also spread over the \nforecast area early, which could moderate temperatures somewhat on\nSunday morning. For now, terrain distinctions were introduced in \nminTs for roughly the western two thirds of the forecast area, \nwhere confidence in widespread cloud cover is lower. Ridge/valley \nsplit development appears less likely farther east, where cloud \ncover is favored to be thicker, although this again depends on the\ntrack of the coastal low. This cloud cover could also suppress \nhighs on Sunday afternoon, although given uncertainty regarding \ncloud cover, baseline NBM MaxTs were maintained for now. Rain \nchances look to increase more meaningfully late Sunday into early \nMonday as the cold front approaches, with chance PoPs across the \nentire forecast area. Even so, QPF should remain low; frontal \nforcing will be relatively weak, instability will be nonexistent, \nand the antecedent air mass will still be fairly dry, with a \nrelatively narrow moisture axis to work with. These factors, in \naddition to the progressive nature of this system, mean that most \nareas will struggle to receive more than 0.1 inch of rain, not \nnearly enough to alleviate drought conditions in our southwestern \ncounties. \n\nBehind the front, CAA via northwesterly winds will end our recent\nperiod of above average temperatures. Highs on Tuesday will \nactually trend below average, in the upper 40s to low 50s across \nthe forecast area, even as sunshine returns to the forecast, \ncourtesy of building midlevel ridging upstream. By Wednesday, \nmodel spread increases, with significant differences in the \npositioning and nature of the next system; however, there is a \ngood ensemble signal that the next chance for precipitation will \narrive by midweek as the next trough approaches, accompanied by \nbelow average temperatures.",
